Lua 5.1.5 patched to behave like LuaJIT 2.1 for KeeperFX's scripts, for the
WebAssembly build (LuaJIT has no WebAssembly backend). Applied by
web/build-deps.sh with `patch -p1` in the Lua source tree; everything here is
something stock LuaJIT 2.1 (as KeeperFX's releases link it) also has:

  - goto and ::labels:: with LuaJIT's rules (lparser.c, llex.c, ldo.c): 'goto'
    is only a statement when a name follows it; a jump out of the scope of
    captured locals closes their upvalues through OP_JMP's A field, as in
    Lua 5.2 (lcode.c, lvm.c, ldebug.c's bytecode check).
  - math.random/randomseed: LuaJIT's Tausworthe generator and range mapping,
    so a seed gives the same numbers as desktop KeeperFX, kept per Lua state
    instead of the C library's rand() shared with the engine (lmathlib.c).
  - Lua 5.2/5.3 additions LuaJIT has: load(chunk, name, mode, env) and
    loadfile(name, mode, env), xpcall(f, msgh, ...), package.searchpath,
    table.move, coroutine.isyieldable, math.log(x, base), string.rep(s, n, sep),
    string.format %a and %s through __tostring, the %g pattern class, \x \z
    \u{...} escapes, hexadecimal fractions/exponents (0x.8p1), UTF-8 in names.
  - LuaJIT's require "table.new" and "table.clear" modules.
  - "nan" for every NaN, %c keeps a NUL byte, as LuaJIT prints them.
  - C API used by KeeperFX: LUA_OK, luaL_setfuncs, luaL_newlib(table),
    luaopen_bit (LuaBitOp, linked in by build-deps.sh).

Not here: LuaJIT's ffi and jit modules (KeeperFX does not open them on the
desktop either), string.buffer, and 64-bit integer literals (1LL, which need
ffi). Lua 5.1 bytecode is not LuaJIT bytecode.

